<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma Huan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1380–c. 1460</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ma Huan was a Chinese Muslim interpreter and traveller who accompanied three of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-treasure-voyages">Ming treasure voyages</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> in the early fifteenth century. His book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingya-shenglan">Yingya Shenglan</a>, &quot;The Overall Survey of the Ocean's Shores&quot;, completed around 1451, describes the countries visited, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and is a major source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> world of his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ma-huan.jpg" alt="Ma Huan" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ma Huan (馬歡) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yingya_Shenglan_(Jilu_Huiban),_first_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ma Huan, courtesy name Zongdao, wrote under the pen name Mountain-woodcutter. He was born in Kuaiji Commandery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, an area within the modern borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a>. He knew several Classical Chinese and Buddhist texts and learned Arabic so that he could serve as a translator on the voyages of Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> to the Western Oceans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On the expedition that sailed in 1413 he went south and west into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, Siam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>. His voyage of 1421 took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, Aru, Sumatra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, Kochi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, Zufar and Hormuz. The voyage of 1431 brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> and Calicut, and from Calicut the eunuch Hong Bao sent him as an emissary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>.</p><p>Throughout the voyages Ma Huan took notes on geography, politics, weather, the environment, trade, local customs and even the punishment of criminals. He began writing on the way home from his first voyage and had a first draft ready around 1416, which he enlarged and revised on the later voyages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingya-shenglan">Yingya Shenglan</a> circulated in many printed and hand-copied editions under the Ming and Qing dynasties. The historian Feng Chengjun edited and annotated a printed text, and a newer edition based on Ming hand copies was published by Ocean Publishing House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. J. V. G. Mills's annotated English translation, based on Feng's edition, was published by the Hakluyt Society in 1970 and reprinted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in 1997.</p><p>Sinologists treat the book as a primary source for Ming naval exploration and for the history of South East Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Scholars who have studied Ma Huan include J. J. L. Duyvendak, F. Hirth, Paul Pelliot, Xiang Da and Mills.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ma Huan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta, who visited eastern Bengal in 1346 shortly before unification, remarked on the abundance and cheapness of its food, and the Chinese interpreter <b>Ma Huan</b>, who accompanied the Ming fleets in the early fifteenth century, described its markets and its silver coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Much of what is known comes from the court poem Nagarakretagama (1365), from inscriptions, and from foreign visitors: Odoric of Pordenone, Ibn Battuta and, in 1413, <b>Ma Huan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30,120.58&amp;year=1432" aria-label="Shaoxing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7683 1068 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ma Huan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8133 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8133 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30,120.58&amp;year=1432">What was here in 1432? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
